WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.419
Dockerfiles and build definitions show how
an image is created.

2
00:00:06.499 --> 00:00:09.678
Review begins with the base image.

3
00:00:09.758 --> 00:00:15.457
Is it maintained, trusted, and appropriate
for the workload?

4
00:00:15.537 --> 00:00:20.689
Is the version pinned enough to make
builds repeatable?

5
00:00:20.769 --> 00:00:23.838
Are package sources controlled?

6
00:00:23.918 --> 00:00:31.920
A weak base image choice can create
recurring vulnerability and maintenance problems

7
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:35.726
even when the application code is sound.

8
00:00:35.806 --> 00:00:38.766
Build steps can introduce risk.

9
00:00:38.846 --> 00:00:45.313
Package installation may add unnecessary
tools, shells, compilers,

10
00:00:45.393 --> 00:00:50.215
or network utilities that are not needed
at runtime.

11
00:00:50.295 --> 00:00:55.666
Copied files may include source, test
data, credentials,

12
00:00:55.746 --> 00:01:01.336
or local configuration that should not be
in the final image.

13
00:01:01.416 --> 00:01:07.445
Multi-stage builds can reduce runtime
footprint when used well,

14
00:01:07.525 --> 00:01:13.882
but the final stage still needs review for
what it actually contains.

15
00:01:13.962 --> 00:01:17.908
Build secrets deserve special attention.

16
00:01:17.988 --> 00:01:24.455
Secrets can be accidentally baked into
image layers, written to logs,

17
00:01:24.535 --> 00:01:31.769
stored in package manager configuration,
or left in intermediate artifacts.

18
00:01:31.849 --> 00:01:39.741
The review should look for patterns that
separate build-time credentials from final

19
00:01:39.821 --> 00:01:47.275
runtime images and confirm that secret
handling aligns with the organization's

20
00:01:47.355 --> 00:01:50.205
approved pipeline practices.

21
00:01:50.285 --> 00:01:54.559
AI can assist by reading Dockerfile
patterns,

22
00:01:54.639 --> 00:02:02.422
explaining hardening opportunities, and
generating a checklist for human review.

23
00:02:02.502 --> 00:02:07.873
For example, it can flag questions about
pinned versions,

24
00:02:07.953 --> 00:02:15.296
non-root users, unnecessary packages,
copied directories, or health checks.

25
00:02:15.376 --> 00:02:21.624
The reviewer must verify each suggestion
against the actual build,

26
00:02:21.704 --> 00:02:28.500
the application requirements, and the
team's operational constraints.
